      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(node-host): handle stdout/stderr stream errors in runCommand",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\n`runCommand` in `src/node-host/invoke.ts` captures child-process stdout/stderr but only attaches `\"data\"` listeners. If either stream emits an `\"error\"` event (e.g. EPIPE after the child exits abruptly, or stream destruction during process teardown), the error is unhandled and can crash the node-host process.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nAdded `\"error\"` listeners to `child.stdout` and `child.stderr` that record the stream error, terminate the child, and let the `exit` handler finalize with the error message. This keeps child exit/close authoritative for `system.run` completion while still preventing the unhandled stream error from crashing the node-host process.\n\n## User Impact\n\nNode-host `system.run` invocations that previously could crash on stream teardown now return a failed result with the error message instead of taking down the process.\n\n## Evidence\n\nRegression tests:\n\n```bash\npnpm test src/node-host/invoke.run-command.test.ts\n```\n\n```\nTest Files  1 passed (1)\n     Tests  2 passed (2)\n```\n\nReal behavior proof:\n\n```bash\nnode_modules/.bin/tsx scripts/proof/node-host-run-command-stream-errors.mts\n```\n\n```\n=== Proof: node-host runCommand stream error handling ===\n\nResult: success=false, error=stdout read failed\n\nPASS: stream errors were caught and runCommand returned a failed result.\n```",
        "branch": "fix/node-host-run-command-stream-errors",
